  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an integer N.\nYour task is to split the digits of N into two non-empty numbers A and B, following these rules:\n\n✅ Rules\n1. Every digit of N must go to either A or B\n\nYou are not allowed to discard or repeat digits.\n\n2. The relative order of digits assigned to A must match their order in N\n\nExample:\nIf N = 3251, and you choose digits 3 → 5 → 1 for A,\nthen A = 351 is valid.\n\nBut A = 315 is not valid.\n3. The same rule applies to B\n\nDigits assigned to B must also appear in the original order.\n\nSo for N = 3251:\n\nA = 3,5, B = 2,1 → ✔ valid\n\nA = 3, B = 2,5,1 → ✔ valid\n\nA = 3, B = 2,1,5 → ❌ invalid (order broken)\n\n4. Both A and B must be non-empty\n\nNo empty number allowed.\n\n5. A and B are normal base-10 integers\n\nLeading zeros are allowed during formation but the number becomes normal:\n00 → 0, 01 → 1, etc.\n\nAmong all valid (A, B) pairs, compute:\n\nA XOR B\nReturn the maximum possible value.",
    "constraints": [
      "0 to 10^6"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Integer",
    "outputFormat": "Integer",
    "notes": [],
